INSERT INTO "weekly_recaps" ("id","season","week","league_id","draft_title","draft_subtitle","draft_recap_json","draft_packet_json","draft_ai_meta_json","draft_generated_at","draft_generated_by","published_title","published_subtitle","published_recap_json","published_packet_json","published_ai_meta_json","published_at","published_by","created_at","updated_at") VALUES(1,2025,8,'1253040502786228224','Dunedin Takes the Throne as Trades Fly','Dagobah posts the week’s biggest number, Amherst vaults four spots, and Nakatomi spends $60 trying to stop the leak.','{"title":"Dunedin Takes the Throne as Trades Fly","subtitle":"Dagobah posts the week’s biggest number, Amherst vaults four spots, and Nakatomi spends $60 trying to stop the leak.","opening":"The Saskatchewan Mounties spent the week sitting in first place, then put up 94.08 points, went 0-2, and watched Dunedin Homers stroll past them into the penthouse. That is the Irving Champions League’s two-decision format at its most rude: lose the matchup, miss the top half, lose two spots. Dunedin did the opposite, rolling Salem 128.66-71.96 for a 2-0 week and a 12-4 record. Meanwhile, the league processed 10 trades, $146 in FAAB, and enough roster shuffling to make the transaction page look like it needed a fire marshal. The standings are tightening; the waiver wire is getting expensive; and the Mounties have company at the top now.","matchupRecaps":[{"matchupId":7,"featured":false,"headline":"Taylor Leads Ultimate City’s Clean Sweep","body":"Ultimate City Warriors handled Tallahassee Tribe 123.06-99.22 behind Jonathan Taylor’s 36.4-point hammer and Daniel Jones’ 23.18. Saquon Barkley gave Tallahassee 31.4 and C.J. Stroud added 22.72, but an Atlanta D/ST zero is a rough accessory in a 23.84-point loss. The Warriors’ 2-0 week lifts them to second at 11-5."},{"matchupId":3,"featured":true,"headline":"Dagobah Wins the Week’s Heavyweight Bout","body":"Dagobah Lightsabres did not merely beat Lehigh Crucible; they survived the week’s highest-scoring loser while posting its highest score, 143.72-128.82. Lehigh got monster work from James Cook (33.6), Tucker Kraft (29.8) and Josh Allen (23.22), yet also received a perfectly useless zero from Quentin Johnston. Dagobah countered with 28 from Tampa Bay D/ST, 24 from Chase Brown and 20 from Chris Boswell—a lineup that got production from the places fantasy usually hides it. The Lightsabres earn the BDE badge, move to 10-6, and remain fourth only because the top of this league is packed tighter than a bad group chat. Lehigh’s 128.82 was good enough for a top-half result, but losing this particular shootout leaves it 8-8 and seventh. Sometimes a great score gets you a point. Sometimes it gets you The Ides and a headache."},{"matchupId":1,"featured":false,"headline":"Dunedin Uses Salem as a Stepladder","body":"Dunedin Homers climbed into first by flattening Salem Hipsterjacks 128.66-71.96. Patrick Mahomes, Michael Pittman and Derrick Henry supplied the useful bits; Salem’s 24.64 from RJ Harvey was not nearly enough to cover a lineup with Isaiah Bond at zero. The Homers went 2-0 to reach 12-4. Salem went 0-2 and remains 14th at 2-14."},{"matchupId":2,"featured":false,"headline":"Rebel Radio Escapes Nakatomi’s Regret","body":"Rebel Radio Lone Rangers survived 85.02-78.94, with Rashee Rice’s 21 points doing the loudest work. Nakatomi’s loss comes with the kind of footnote that keeps a manager staring at the lineup page: Isaiah Davis scored 15.4 on the bench while Jordan Mason started for 1.1. The calculated swap would have made it 93.24-85.02. Oof."},{"matchupId":5,"featured":false,"headline":"Amherst Wins by Three and a Half","body":"Amherst Union’s four-place climb began with a 98.76-95.26 squeaker over Jacksonville Vincitori. Breece Hall’s 31.86 carried Amherst past its Cincinnati D/ST’s -4 disaster. Jacksonville got 28.3 from Jordan Love and 19 from Buffalo D/ST, but Hunter Henry’s 7.2 sat behind T.J. Hockenson’s 3.6; the league’s hindsight calculation says that swap flips it by a tenth."},{"matchupId":6,"featured":false,"headline":"Clearwater Survives the One-Point Nuisance","body":"Clearwater HenryPussycats beat Milford Jayhawks 92.50-90.72 despite receiving zero from Minnesota D/ST. Drake Maye scored 27.28, and it barely held. Milford benched Tyjae Spears’ 15.7 while Bijan Robinson scored 4.3; that calculated replacement turns the loss into a 102.12-92.50 win. Instead, Milford gets an 0-2 week and falls from seventh to 10th."},{"matchupId":4,"featured":false,"headline":"Kodachromes Knock the Mounties Off First","body":"Kansas City Kodachromes beat Saskatchewan Mounties 107.70-94.08, with Jalen Hurts scoring 25.36. The Mounties had a brutal alternate universe available: Jaxson Dart’s 19.42 on the bench instead of Baker Mayfield’s 4.08 would have produced a 109.42-107.70 win. Reality chose the version where Saskatchewan went 0-2 and surrendered first place."}],"waiverWire":{"headline":"Nakatomi Pays for Running Back Insurance","body":"Kevin Flanagan’s Nakatomi Plaza CC accounted for the week’s biggest waiver splash, paying $50 for Tank Bigsby and another $10 for J.J. McCarthy. That was $60 of the league’s $146 total, and it came after Nakatomi’s 0-2 week dropped the club to 5-11. Lehigh Crucible was the other major bidder, spending $33 on Samaje Perine and $27 on Chimere Dike. Tallahassee paid $11 for Devin Singletary, while Kansas City made two smaller position-specific moves, adding Juwan Johnson and New Orleans D/ST for a combined $11. The cheap end of the market also produced a pair of kicker claims by first-place Dunedin.","notableClaims":[{"teamName":"Nakatomi Plaza CC","players":["Tank Bigsby"],"faab":50,"commentary":"The week’s biggest single bid, by a distance."},{"teamName":"Lehigh Crucible","players":["Samaje Perine"],"faab":33,"commentary":"Lehigh paid real money for a back after the shootout loss."},{"teamName":"Lehigh Crucible","players":["Chimere Dike"],"faab":27,"commentary":"A second substantial bid made Lehigh the other major spender."},{"teamName":"Tallahassee Tribe","players":["Devin Singletary"],"faab":11,"commentary":"Tallahassee swapped out Cade Otton and spent $11 on a running back."}]},"tradeDesk":{"headline":"Ten Deals and a Lot of 2026 Money","body":"The trade desk was not open this week; it was a revolving door. Every one of the 10 posted deals included confirmed 2026 capital, from a two-dollar Bhayshul Tuten transaction to Dunedin’s $75 payment for Christian McCaffrey. The immediate result is a league where half the rosters appear to have been passed around the room. Nobody can accuse this bunch of letting a quiet Tuesday remain quiet.","items":[{"transactionId":"1288957966485032960","headline":"Defenses Swap, Five Dollars Follow","body":"Ultimate City sent Chicago Bears D/ST to Dunedin for Houston Texans D/ST, with Dunedin also sending $5 in 2026 capital to the Warriors."},{"transactionId":"1288663247754371072","headline":"Amherst Lands Stroud in Major Package","body":"Amherst acquired C.J. Stroud; Tallahassee received Aaron Rodgers, Garrett Wilson and Breece Hall. Tallahassee also sent $23 in 2026 capital to Amherst."},{"transactionId":"1288187465777057792","headline":"Patriots D/ST Costs Six","body":"Lehigh acquired New England Patriots D/ST from Nakatomi and sent $6 in 2026 capital the other way."},{"transactionId":"1287919084901519360","headline":"Dunedin Pays for McCaffrey","body":"Dunedin landed Christian McCaffrey from Jacksonville for Rico Dowdle and $75 in 2026 capital."},{"transactionId":"1287406641431470080","headline":"Geno and Rhamondre Change Addresses","body":"Jacksonville acquired Rhamondre Stevenson while Salem received Geno Smith. Jacksonville sent $5 in 2026 capital to Salem."},{"transactionId":"1287235634917834753","headline":"Herbert Heads to Rebel Radio","body":"Rebel Radio acquired Justin Herbert; Nakatomi received Cooper Kupp and $4 in 2026 capital."},{"transactionId":"1287229663910850560","headline":"Clearwater Buys Bhayshul Tuten","body":"Clearwater acquired Bhayshul Tuten from Dagobah for $2 in 2026 capital."},{"transactionId":"1286935569032564736","headline":"Dagobah Gets Brown and Egbuka","body":"Dagobah acquired Chase Brown and Emeka Egbuka, while Nakatomi received Rome Odunze and $10 in 2026 capital."},{"transactionId":"1286917701884710912","headline":"Tucker Kraft Goes to Lehigh","body":"Lehigh acquired Tucker Kraft from Nakatomi for Kyle Pitts and $18 in 2026 capital."},{"transactionId":"1286826780837019648","headline":"Milford Sends Jones to Kansas City","body":"Kansas City acquired Aaron Jones; Milford received Trey McBride, Tyjae Spears and $20 in 2026 capital."}]},"standings":{"headline":"First Place Changes Hands; Amherst Leaps","body":"Dunedin’s 2-0 week pushed the Homers from second to first at 12-4, while Ultimate City’s 2-0 rose them to second at 11-5. Saskatchewan had entered the week 10-4 and first; a 94.08 score fell below the 97.01 median, so the Mounties took both losses and fell to third at 10-6. Dagobah matched that 10-6 record after its 143.72-point outburst but stayed fourth. The cleanest rise belongs to Amherst: a 2-0 week moved the Union from ninth to fifth, 9-7. Milford had the opposite experience, dropping three places to 10th after its 0-2 stumble."},"awards":{"headline":"Week 8’s Hardware, Regrets, and Zeroes","items":[{"title":"BDE / The Ides","teamName":"Dagobah Lightsabres and Lehigh Crucible","body":"Dagobah’s 143.72 was the league’s top score. Lehigh’s 128.82 was the top losing score, which is the fantasy equivalent of bringing the good casserole to the wrong potluck."},{"title":"The Sucko","teamName":"Salem Hipsterjacks","body":"Salem’s 71.96 was Week 8’s low score. RJ Harvey and Kimani Vidal tried to keep the lights on, but the building was already condemned."},{"title":"Zero Hour","teamName":"Amherst Union, Clearwater HenryPussycats, and Tallahassee Tribe","body":"Amherst started Cincinnati D/ST for 0.00, Clearwater got 0.00 from Minnesota D/ST, and Tallahassee got 0.00 from Atlanta D/ST. Amherst still won; Clearwater still won; Tallahassee did not. Defense remains a very funny position."},{"title":"Cap’n Hindsight","teamName":"Nakatomi Plaza CC","body":"Isaiah Davis scored 15.4 on Nakatomi’s bench while Jordan Mason scored 1.1 in the lineup. The league’s calculated swap turns a 85.02-78.94 loss into a 93.24-85.02 win."},{"title":"Cap’n Hindsight","teamName":"Milford Jayhawks","body":"Tyjae Spears’ 15.7 bench points over Bijan Robinson’s 4.3 would have turned Milford’s 1.78-point loss into a 102.12-92.50 victory. That is not a minor oversight; that is a whole different Sunday."},{"title":"Cap’n Hindsight","teamName":"Saskatchewan Mounties","body":"Jaxson Dart’s 19.42 was parked behind Baker Mayfield’s 4.08. The calculated 15.34-point gain would have beaten Kansas City and kept Saskatchewan from its 0-2 tumble out of first."}]},"closing":"Dunedin has first place, Ultimate City is right behind, and four teams are sitting at either 10-6 or 9-7. Meanwhile, Nakatomi has spent $60 on waivers and still has Isaiah Davis staring back from the Week 8 bench. That lineup will not stop haunting people anytime soon."}','{"schemaVersion":1,"generatedAt":"2026-08-14T19:55:43.041Z","season":2025,"week":8,"league":{"id":"1253040502786228224","name":"Irving Champions League"},"summary":{"matchupCount":7,"teamCount":14,"transactionCount":66,"waiverCount":10,"freeAgentCount":46,"tradeCount":10,"commissionerMoveCount":0,"totalAdds":63,"totalDrops":61,"faabSpent":146},"highlights":{"highestCombined":{"matchupId":3,"left":{"rosterId":2,"teamName":"Lehigh Crucible","managerName":"Dave Oliverio","managerSlug":"lehigh-crucible","score":128.82,"starters":[{"id":"4984","name":"Josh Allen","position":"QB","nflTeam":"BUF","fantasyPoints":23.22},{"id":"8138","name":"James Cook","position":"RB","nflTeam":"BUF","fantasyPoints":33.6},{"id":"12481","name":"Cam Skattebo","position":"RB","nflTeam":"NYG","fantasyPoints":9.5},{"id":"975